WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.680
Four locks and four keys.

2
00:00:05.791 --> 00:00:08.904
A coloured square means this

3
00:00:09.015 --> 00:00:11.239
key opens this lock.

4
00:00:11.239 --> 00:00:13.352
Lock one: key two.

5
00:00:13.352 --> 00:00:16.687
Lock two: keys one and three.

6
00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:19.244
Lock three: key three.

7
00:00:19.244 --> 00:00:22.580
Lock four: keys two and four.

8
00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:26.605
Here are two sentences with the same words.

9
00:00:26.605 --> 00:00:29.682
Every lock has a key that opens it.

10
00:00:29.682 --> 00:00:32.930
There is a key that opens every lock.

11
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.560
Do they say the same thing?

12
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.080
Pause and decide.

13
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.438
The first sentence says: for every lock

14
00:00:44.438 --> 00:00:46.327
there exists a key.

15
00:00:46.327 --> 00:00:48.216
So check every row.

16
00:00:48.216 --> 00:00:50.294
Row one has a square.

17
00:00:50.294 --> 00:00:53.128
Row two, row three, row four

18
00:00:53.128 --> 00:00:55.300
each one has a square.

19
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:56.098
True.

20
00:00:56.098 --> 00:01:00.043
And the key may be different for each lock

21
00:01:00.043 --> 00:01:03.450
key two, key one, key three, key two.

22
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.112
The second sentence says

23
00:01:06.112 --> 00:01:09.026
there exists a key, for all locks.

24
00:01:09.026 --> 00:01:11.190
That key is chosen first

25
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.021
and then it must open every lock

26
00:01:14.021 --> 00:01:15.270
a full column.

27
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.134
Key one misses lock one.

28
00:01:18.134 --> 00:01:20.753
Key two misses lock two.

29
00:01:20.753 --> 00:01:23.582
Key three misses lock one

30
00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:25.887
and so does key four.

31
00:01:25.887 --> 00:01:27.877
No column is full.

32
00:01:27.877 --> 00:01:31.020
The second sentence is false.

33
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.149
Same words, different order

34
00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:35.879
different meaning.

35
00:01:35.879 --> 00:01:38.246
In for all, there exists

36
00:01:38.246 --> 00:01:41.159
the key may depend on the lock.

37
00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:43.526
In there exists, for all

38
00:01:43.526 --> 00:01:47.440
one key is fixed before any lock is named.

39
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.114
One direction always holds.

40
00:01:50.114 --> 00:01:53.214
Imagine key four opened every lock

41
00:01:53.214 --> 00:01:54.505
a full column.

42
00:01:54.505 --> 00:01:57.604
Then every row would have a square

43
00:01:57.604 --> 00:01:58.982
in that column.

44
00:01:58.982 --> 00:02:02.684
So there exists, for all, implies for all

45
00:02:02.684 --> 00:02:03.889
there exists.

46
00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.955
But the converse fails

47
00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:08.710
this table is a counterexample.

48
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.370
With numbers

49
00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.694
for every integer x there is a y

50
00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:16.616
with x plus y equal to zero.

51
00:02:16.616 --> 00:02:19.134
Take y equal to minus x.

52
00:02:19.134 --> 00:02:22.660
But no single y works for every x.

53
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.424
To negate a sentence with two quantifiers

54
00:02:27.424 --> 00:02:28.520
flip both.

55
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.804
Not: there is a key that opens every lock.

56
00:02:32.804 --> 00:02:35.693
That becomes: for every key

57
00:02:35.693 --> 00:02:39.080
there is a lock it does not open.

58
00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:40.605
In the table

59
00:02:40.605 --> 00:02:43.677
every column has an empty square

60
00:02:43.677 --> 00:02:47.202
lock one for keys one, three and four

61
00:02:47.202 --> 00:02:49.190
lock two for key two.

62
00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.985
Now find the error.

63
00:02:51.985 --> 00:02:57.115
Claim: there is a largest natural number m

64
00:02:57.115 --> 00:02:59.913
with every n at most m.

65
00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:04.111
Proof: let n be any natural number

66
00:03:04.111 --> 00:03:07.842
and take m equal to n plus one.

67
00:03:07.842 --> 00:03:10.290
Then n is at most m.

68
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.602
The error: m was chosen after n

69
00:03:13.602 --> 00:03:16.009
so it moves when n moves.

70
00:03:16.009 --> 00:03:19.711
That proves: for every n there is an m.

71
00:03:19.711 --> 00:03:21.840
A different statement.

72
00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.281
For the real claim, m must be fixed first.

73
00:03:26.281 --> 00:03:30.142
And then n equal to m plus one is bigger.

74
00:03:30.142 --> 00:03:31.980
The claim is false.

75
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.043
Now a new kind of proof.

76
00:03:35.043 --> 00:03:38.353
Claim: two to the n, minus one

77
00:03:38.353 --> 00:03:42.697
equals one plus two plus four, and so on

78
00:03:42.697 --> 00:03:45.800
up to two to the n minus one.

79
00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.760
A combinatorial proof asks

80
00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:51.069
one counting question

81
00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:53.880
and answers it in two ways.

82
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.285
Our question

83
00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:59.702
how many nonempty subsets does the set one

84
00:03:59.702 --> 00:04:01.710
two, up to n, have?

85
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:06.229
For n equals four, here are all the subsets

86
00:04:06.229 --> 00:04:09.260
in binary order, four to a row.

87
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.410
Left side first.

88
00:04:11.508 --> 00:04:13.962
A set with n elements has

89
00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.494
two to the n subsets: sixteen here.

90
00:04:17.494 --> 00:04:19.948
Leave out the empty set

91
00:04:19.948 --> 00:04:23.481
and fifteen are left: two to the n

92
00:04:23.481 --> 00:04:24.560
minus one.

93
00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.031
Right side

94
00:04:26.031 --> 00:04:29.515
sort the subsets by their largest element.

95
00:04:29.515 --> 00:04:32.674
Largest element one: just the set one.

96
00:04:32.674 --> 00:04:35.428
Largest element two: two subsets.

97
00:04:35.428 --> 00:04:38.425
Largest element three: four subsets

98
00:04:38.425 --> 00:04:39.722
the second row.

99
00:04:39.722 --> 00:04:42.719
Largest element four: eight subsets

100
00:04:42.719 --> 00:04:44.420
the bottom two rows.

101
00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.442
Why two to the k minus one?

102
00:04:47.442 --> 00:04:51.202
A subset with largest element k is k

103
00:04:51.301 --> 00:04:53.675
together with any subset

104
00:04:53.774 --> 00:04:56.050
of the numbers below k.

105
00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.692
Each nonempty subset has exactly

106
00:04:59.788 --> 00:05:01.702
one largest element

107
00:05:01.702 --> 00:05:04.668
so it is counted exactly once.

108
00:05:04.764 --> 00:05:07.348
One plus two plus four plus

109
00:05:07.443 --> 00:05:09.070
eight is fifteen.

110
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.183
Both sides count the same set

111
00:05:12.183 --> 00:05:15.130
so they are equal, for every n.

112
00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:19.275
That is a combinatorial proof: one question

113
00:05:19.275 --> 00:05:20.380
two counts.

114
00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.731
So: read quantifiers from left to right.

115
00:05:24.731 --> 00:05:28.501
There exists, for all, implies for all

116
00:05:28.501 --> 00:05:29.821
there exists

117
00:05:29.821 --> 00:05:33.120
but not in the opposite direction.

118
00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.421
To negate, flip every quantifier.

119
00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.818
And a combinatorial proof answers one

120
00:05:39.908 --> 00:05:42.590
counting question in two ways.

121
00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.957
Try it yourself: count the strings of zeros

122
00:05:47.043 --> 00:05:49.194
ones and twos of length n

123
00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.378
that are not all zeros, in two ways.

124
00:05:52.378 --> 00:05:55.390
You get three to the n, minus one

125
00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:59.176
equals two times one plus three, and so on

126
00:05:59.176 --> 00:06:01.930
up to three to the n minus one.

127
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.459
Next lecture: relations

128
00:06:04.549 --> 00:06:07.182
where pairs like lock and key

129
00:06:07.272 --> 00:06:09.360
become the main object.
